@@s002 Code: Bellman-Ford in Java
@@s003 Shortest paths with negative weights: n − 1 rounds of relaxation over all the edges, and the negative-cycle check with one more round.
@@s004 The relaxation rounds
@@s005 Each round scans all the edges. After n − 1 rounds the distances are final, if there are no negative cycles.
@@s006 The relaxation rounds
@@s007 Infinity as a <code>long</code> divided by 4: adding a negative weight to it does not cause an overflow.
@@s008 n − 1 rounds: a simple path has at most n − 1 edges.
@@s009 Relaxing an edge: if going through u is cheaper, I update v.
@@s010 Syntax notes
@@s011 <code>final</code> on the variable: the value no longer changes after the assignment.
@@s012 Negative weights are not a problem for Java: they are integers like the others.
@@s013 The negative cycle
@@s014 One more round: if a distance still improves, there is a negative cycle reachable from the source.
@@s015 The negative cycle
@@s016 If an edge still improves after n − 1 rounds, there is a negative cycle: the shortest path does not exist.
@@s017 Syntax notes
@@s018 <code>return</code> inside <code>main</code> stops the program.
